WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
<v Mike>Yo-Noid, or How a Yo-Yo Saved New York.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:28.420
<v Mike>And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.060
<v Hosts>I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.600
<v Sean>Yo, Mike. Yeah, yo, Sean.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.160
<v Mike>What's up? How you doing?

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:40.280
<v Mike>You think that the Yo is mandatory as part of the Noid?

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.840
<v Sean>I think with, like, you know, early 90s, Yo is mandatory.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:54.200
<v Sean>Maybe not so much in relation to Noid himself, but I think, like, I was expecting more titles like this.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.760
<v Mike>What do you know about the Noid?

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.800
<v Mike>I didn't know a goddamn thing until this episode came on our radar.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:01.960
<v Mike>And so-

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.660
<v Sean>Now I feel like it, this is some, like, Mandela effect thing.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.840
<v Mike>Yeah. No, I knew about the Noid because he's, like, an-

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.940
<v Mike>He's an annoying, obnoxious character.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:17.200
<v Mike>But here's my whole, here's my, like, get into it real early in the episode.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:21.500
<v Mike>So, like, McDonald's has the Hamburglar, right?

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.420
<v Mike>And, like, he wants to steal-

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:24.900
<v Sean>And Ronald McDonald and so many others.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:26.680
<v Mike>Yeah, yeah. So many other mascots.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:34.680
<v Mike>But I'm bringing up the Hamburglar specifically because he wants to, like, steal the Big Mac because it's delicious.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:35.980
<v Mike>You know what I'm saying?

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:45.060
<v Sean>So you mean, like- Yeah. You're talking about the built-in villains and antagonists of your marketing campaign.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:58.020
<v Mike>Right, right, right, right. But, like, even though the Hamburglar is, first off, like, a 10 out of 10 name, one of the best marketing gimmicks of all time, but, like, he still loves the McDonald's burger.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:02.860
<v Mike>He's not stealing the burgers because he secretly works for Burger King and hates McDonald's.

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:06.300
<v Mike>The Noid hates Domino's.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.520
<v Mike>Like, it's really strange, right?

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:22.600
<v Sean>Yeah, I guess it's more so just an agent of chaos than it is anybody that has an opinion on the food from what I was able to gather.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.960
<v Sean>Yes, he's not- But then again, I didn't really do that much gathering.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.700
<v Mike>He's not a real entity.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:36.480
<v Mike>He's, like, what could go wrong to make your delivery not be delivered in time because Domino's was, like, 30 minutes or less, you know?

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.080
<v Mike>And so it's like- So he's Michael Madsen.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.220
<v Mike>Yes. And the Noid would make you annoyed, right?

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:46.220
<v Mike>Like, it's insufferable.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:49.880
<v Mike>So I guess that's the Noid part of it is he's called that because-

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:52.280
<v Sean>I thought it would be, like, the paranoia.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:53.740
<v Sean>I guess I don't really know.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.100
<v Sean>I think it's for annoyed, if you want me to be honest.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.540
<v Mike>I don't have, like, a citation for that.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:07.880
<v Sean>I think he stands for, in our society, certain patterns, you know?

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:13.600
<v Joe>And NOI, like, sort of like a return on investment.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:43.580
<v Mike>Got it.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:46.360
<v Mike>There's a CBS cartoon about the Noid.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.380
<v Mike>I don't think it ever saw the light of day.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.460
<v Mike>I couldn't find, like, a pilot for it or anything, but it was planned.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.340
<v Mike>And then there's actual, like, Domino's merchandise with the Noid.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:58.280
<v Sean>So- Do we know why he was a rabbit?

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.540
<v Mike>Why he wears those ears?

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:02.800
<v Sean>Oh, he just wears the ears.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:07.180
<v Mike>Do we know that if he pulled them off that he would have giant bunny ears?

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:08.240
<v Mike>Or do we just assume-

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.900
<v Sean>I guess I don't know. I thought maybe that was just his skin.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:14.920
<v Mike>And I think it's just, like, again, because he's supposed to just be irritating to look at.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:26.520
<v Mike>Yeah. Now, in 1989, a Georgia man named Kenneth Lamar did hold two Domino's employees hostage.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:28.280
<v Mike>His name was Noid.

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:29.420
<v Mike>Yeah. Sorry.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:30.400
<v Mike>I left out the-

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:31.760
<v Mike>I was saving that for the punchline.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:36.220
<v Mike>But, like, his name was Kenneth Lamar Noid.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.020
<v Mike>And he held two Domino's employees hostage.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:40.420
<v Mike>He surrendered eventually.

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:41.840
<v Mike>Nobody was hurt or anything.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:47.040
<v Mike>And there is a conspiracy that this is what got the Noid off the air.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.880
<v Sean>Well, I did also find this out.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:56.660
<v Sean>Which I, again, part of why I thought this was some kind of, like, Mandela effect situation.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:58.360
<v Sean>Because it's like, I would have-

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:00.040
<v Sean>I feel like I would have known about this.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:01.420
<v Sean>But apparently I didn't.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:08.220
<v Sean>And also, I think, like, they only retired the Noid, like, five years after this.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:12.440
<v Sean>So, it doesn't hold that much water.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:17.020
<v Sean>I think hopefully maybe in the 90s people were just like, this has been weird the whole time.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:19.040
<v Sean>And we've just kind of had enough of it.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:24.080
<v Sean>But one thing I want to push back on is you've-

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:26.280
<v Joe>You've said that he surrendered.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:37.280
<v Sean>Whereas the story that I read goes that he was sort of outsmarted by his captors who made him pizza.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:41.040
<v Sean>And then he, like, put the gun down and they just ran away.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:44.680
<v Mike>So, he surrendered to the pizza.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:48.920
<v Sean>Yes. He surrendered to his cravings for Domino's.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:53.940
<v Sean>Which, if you ask me, sounds like some pretty awesome marketing for your pizza.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:06:03.260
<v Sean>If, like, even would-be hostage takers can't really-

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:05.660
<v Sean>Can't say no to the taste.

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:07.900
<v Sean>But I just missed opportunities.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:11.400
<v Mike>Where they were getting briefed on it, you know, like, up at Domino's HQ.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:13.560
<v Mike>And they were like, you know, please don't die.

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:17.160
<v Mike>Please don't die. And then they heard that the pizza was so good that he surrendered.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:19.720
<v Mike>And they were like, we got to do something about this.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.700
<v Mike>The Noid did come back, though, a few times.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:26.540
<v Mike>2011 for the 25th birthday.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:33.000
<v Mike>He got, like, a Facebook video game, web game, called Noid's Super Pizza Shootout.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:35.200
<v Mike>Whoa. Yeah.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.420
<v Sean>That's a little strange considering.

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:39.600
<v Sean>Close to the hostage incident.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.700
<v Mike>And then in 2016, Domino's got a pinball machine.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:44.960
<v Mike>And the Noid's in it.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:46.780
<v Mike>Like, it's not, like, all about him, but he's in it.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:48.660
<v Mike>And so, like, another reference to him.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:51.680
<v Mike>But for the most part, like, I don't know.

00:06:53.060 --> 00:07:01.140
<v Mike>Domino's has had long enough time away from him that I don't think anyone other than the people who grew up with the Noid associate the two together.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:02.920
<v Sean>I sure as hell didn't.

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:11.200
<v Sean>Right. But I guess just a question a little further outside the scope of the video game in front of us.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:12.680
<v Sean>Do you like Domino's pizza?

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.140
<v Mike>Okay. So this is a great question.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:17.340
<v Mike>And it is one I planned to answer.

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:18.820
<v Mike>So I have a good response here.

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:28.680
<v Mike>And for the longest time, especially growing up on Long Island and going into the city, like, that's where the best pizza in the world is.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:36.060
<v Mike>And I never saw a need to have the Domino's, Papa John's, Pizza Hut, Little Cece's, whatever.

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:37.320
<v Mike>Whatever there is out there.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:40.120
<v Mike>I didn't feel the need to ever.

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.420
<v Mike>Little Caesars. Little Caesars and Cece's pizza, right?

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:43.680
<v Mike>Yeah. I combined them.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:47.760
<v Mike>There was never a need for that in my life.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:52.780
<v Mike>But my wife does, I would say, love Domino's.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.080
<v Mike>So I had to, like, have it, obviously.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:57.140
<v Mike>Like, it's in my life now.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:02.820
<v Mike>There's actually, like, a yearly tradition around Christmas time where I give her, like, a Domino's gift card and we have it.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:09.080
<v Mike>And the first time I had it, I have to say I was surprised that it's not really pizza.

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:10.440
<v Mike>It's Domino's.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:11.500
<v Mike>It's not the same kind.

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:16.100
<v Mike>It doesn't satisfy the same way a thin New York slice does.

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:23.080
<v Mike>This is, they've kind of, like, carved out their own area of the pizza market where it's like, I get it.

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:27.680
<v Mike>If you're going to Domino's, you don't want that, like, thin crust slice.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:32.360
<v Mike>Yeah, you want Domino's. And so I am not a Domino's hater any longer.

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:33.640
<v Sean>Well, I'm glad to hear that.

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:40.320
<v Sean>I also think that Domino's has its place alongside, you know, I think pizza has its own thing,

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:43.120
<v Sean>too. And they all have a purpose.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:46.300
<v Mike>Now, I do want to bring up another thing about Domino's.

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:50.600
<v Mike>And maybe you can help me with this or maybe you'll just come to the same conclusion I did.

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:58.680
<v Mike>But since I've been eating Domino's, which is probably right around 2014, there has been a deal on the website

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:06.720
<v Mike>where for $20 you get two medium pies, like the garlic knots or whatever they call them.

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:09.020
<v Mike>It's because they're not knots. Cheesy bread. No, no, no.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:10.660
<v Mike>It's like, it's definitely garlic something.

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:12.940
<v Mike>It's like garlic twists. There's garlic Parmesan twists.

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:23.160
<v Mike>Okay. And then you get cinnamon twists, too, with like a cheesecake dipping sauce or whatever, and a two-liter bottle of Coke.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:24.540
<v Mike>And now that's all for $20.

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:30.260
<v Mike>Okay. They eventually get rid of the two-liter bottle of Coke, and it's still the $20.

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:35.000
<v Mike>There's no other promo on that site that even comes close.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:41.940
<v Mike>Like, you might not even be hungry for the whole thing, but for the price difference, I've tried every other combo you can think of.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:42.960
<v Mike>I've tried their other coupons.

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:49.340
<v Mike>There's no reason not to just get two medium pizzas, the garlic thing and the cinnamon thing, and just call it a night.

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:52.920
<v Sean>Yeah, that's probably something like 4,000, 5,000 calories right there.

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:55.700
<v Mike>Yeah, but again, if you're having it at 2 a.m., it don't count.

00:09:57.380 --> 00:09:58.920
<v Sean>You have said this.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:01.100
<v Mike>Offline, I've said this, yeah.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:08.620
<v Sean>I'm just thinking about, like, the price per calorie or calories per dollar, you know?

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:10.180
<v Sean>So that is the deal.

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:11.160
<v Joe>That is a deal right there.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:13.920
<v Mike>You asked why he looks like that.

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:16.020
<v Sean>I do want to know that.

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:17.220
<v Mike>I'm going to put that in your words.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:25.280
<v Mike>Apparently, the red suit with the rabbit-like ears and the big N just read well in claymation.

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:33.260
<v Mike>So they gave him the ears because it made him pop more on older TV sets.

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:35.920
<v Mike>That's the alleged citation that I have here.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.360
<v Mike>I'm not going to investigate the source.

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:39.460
<v Mike>But I guess that makes sense, right?

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:44.900
<v Mike>Like, if he just had, like, a red cap, it wouldn't stand out that much.

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:47.600
<v Mike>Like, he needed a little something extra to mascot him.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:11:00.680
<v Sean>Now, you know, one thing that doesn't particularly read well, and I'm going to loop this into the game itself here, too, is the association with Domino's Pizza.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:12.420
<v Sean>Because, I mean, again, I guess it's part of his whole, like, antagonistic streak that, you know, he does, he's not, he's not the, he isn't representing Domino's.

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:15.060
<v Sean>He's representing what could go wrong in a Domino's delivery.

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:26.080
<v Sean>But even, like, in this game, like, the pizza is, the pizza place that you go to over and over, it doesn't happen to be Domino's.

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:27.040
<v Sean>It's just a pizza place.

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:34.640
<v Joe>Like, it's just, this is a Noid game, and the whole association with Domino's is very much incidental, it seems.

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:42.340
<v Mike>And yet it is kind of funny that every level ends with him being forced to have a pizza-eating competition, something he hates.

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:53.340
<v Mike>He hates pizza, and he and the other Noids that live in this Noid land have to eat pizza, and whoever eats the most moves on to the next round.

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:57.000
<v Mike>There is something poetic about that.

00:11:57.460 --> 00:12:00.180
<v Sean>Very. I'm not sure what kind of poetry it is.

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:06.200
<v Joe>It's, like, more, maybe it's more, like, slam poetry without rhyming.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:08.960
<v Sean>But, you know, it's poetry.

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:17.380
<v Mike>Sean, can a licensed mascot platformer ever earn a seat next to Capcom's original NES greats, like Mega Man?

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:19.120
<v Mike>Like, is there ever a chance?

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:23.680
<v Sean>Like, the quality of this video game?

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:30.200
<v Mike>Not the quality of this video game specifically, but I guess, like, does, so they're creating, like, their own mascots, Capcom, right?

00:12:30.260 --> 00:12:35.580
<v Mike>Like, they are making games with great, memorable IPs that are still being made today.

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:40.580
<v Mike>And then, like, they're also making licensed games at the same time.

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:47.600
<v Mike>And it's, like, do you have a certain judgment about the quality of these just knowing that it's a Domino's tie-in because of the other?

00:12:47.720 --> 00:13:01.020
<v Mike>Like, we are basically, at this point in the show right now in the 1990s, we have become, like, a pop culture podcast because everything is a licensed, like, movie or toy or franchise of some sort.

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:11.700
<v Mike>And, you know, I guess, like, I'm not saying I had a bias going into it, but I certainly didn't expect this to be on the level of Capcom games because of the forced tie-in.

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:25.140
<v Sean>Yeah, I think that the only reason that the last episode that I can think of that even comes close to this would be the spot game.

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:29.620
<v Sean>And I'm pretty sure we also had, like, a McDonald's one, but that...

00:13:29.620 --> 00:13:30.920
<v Sean>We did. We did.

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:33.980
<v Sean>Okay. Um, but I had...

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:43.940
<v Sean>I had... Myself had nostalgia for a spot video game, so I think that kind of disqualifies me from having an actual opinion on the game itself.

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:47.680
<v Sean>But, like, on the quality of any other spot video game.

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:49.640
<v Sean>But, like, this one...

00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:59.740
<v Sean>Having not known what Noid was and then learning that it's Domino's Pizza, I think I did have some prejudice against it as, like,

00:13:59.740 --> 00:14:01.040
<v Sean>Oh, this is gonna be a good game.

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:02.800
<v Sean>Obviously, it has to.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:04.580
<v Sean>It's Domino's Pizza, right?

00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:05.880
<v Sean>No one's gonna think that.

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:09.780
<v Mike>And Capcom basically took a game that they had already made.

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:15.740
<v Mike>I don't know how to say it in Japanese, so it just translates to Masked Ninja Hanamoru.

00:14:16.420 --> 00:14:18.560
<v Mike>And that game just...

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:25.640
<v Mike>It's basically the same game, but it got a complete visual overhaul to fit the Noid and the pizza and all that into it.

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:32.540
<v Mike>But it wasn't like they started from scratch, like, coming up with a Yo-Noid video game.

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:35.020
<v Mike>They basically looked at what they had and then said, like...

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:37.900
<v Mike>I guess, like, they probably went to Domino's or somebody and was like,

00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:40.580
<v Mike>Who wants to make a game with us?

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:41.800
<v Mike>And who wants to pay us money?

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:43.420
<v Mike>And Domino's was like, us!

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:46.460
<v Mike>And then they were like, but did you remember to include our logo anywhere?

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:49.520
<v Sean>Wow, you actually just kind of creeped me out.

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:54.300
<v Sean>Because I was wondering why you kept using Capcom as an example of just like,

00:14:54.300 --> 00:15:01.780
<v Sean>Oh, you mean like, maybe this is a little Mega Man-y, so this is why Mike is bringing it up as a comparison.

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:04.400
<v Joe>Like, no, Capcom made this game.

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:06.340
<v Joe>That's actually...

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:13.900
<v Sean>And I remember now seeing this on the intro screen and it just completely washing over me.

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:18.020
<v Sean>It's actually crazy that they made this game.

00:15:18.020 --> 00:15:25.560
<v Mike>Yeah, now what I understand is that they played a role in the making of this game and were definitely the publisher of the game.

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:33.320
<v Mike>Seems like now Productions might have made the, like, original Japanese game, the Mass Ninja one.

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:35.740
<v Mike>And then Capcom just, like, re-skinned it here.

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:37.400
<v Mike>So it's not a true...

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:42.280
<v Mike>I mean, it is technically a true Capcom game because it's under their, like, wing.

00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:43.500
<v Mike>Yeah, they're umbrella, yeah.

